h the father\uc1 \u8217 ?s chariot. Pelops then killed his confederate by throwing him in the sea. The drowning man cursed Pelops and his future family. }{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \cf0 \par }\pard \plain \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \lisa0 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \af5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \cf0 \par }\pard \plain \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \lisa0 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \af5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \cf0 As a result of the curse, Pelops\uc1 \u8217 ?s two sons, Atreus and Thyestes quarrelled, and Atreus, after inviting Thyestes to a reconciliation banquet, killed Thyestes\uc1 \u8217 ?s sons and served them to him at dinner, revealing what he had done only after Thyestes had already eaten them. Thyestes then fathered a child by his daughter to produce a son, Aegisthus, who could avenge the notorious Thyestean banquet.}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \cf0 \par }\pard \plain \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \lisa0 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \af5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \cf0 \par }\pard \plain \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \lisa0 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \af5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \cf0 When Helen, daughter of Tyndareus, king of Sparta, who had married Menelaus, eloped with Paris to Troy, many of the Greek warrior leaders gathered an army and sailed to Troy to get Helen back, thus launching the Trojan War. The expedition was led by Agamemnon, Menelaus\uc1 \u8217 ?s brother (and a son of Atreus), who sacrificed his eldest daughter, Iphigeneia, in order to appease the gods and allow the army to sail from Greece.}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \cf0 \par }\pard \plain \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \lisa0 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \af5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \cf0 \par }\pard \plain \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \lisa0 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af5 \loch \af5 \lang1033 \langnp1033 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 {\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \cf0 While Agamemnon was at Troy, Aegisthus, Thyestes\uc1 \u8217 ?s son, seduced Agamemnon\uc1 \u8217 ?s wife, Clytaemnestra (a sister of Helen), and as soon as Agamemnon returned from Troy, they murdered him, thus avenging the Thyestean banquet and the killing of Iphigeneia. Orestes, Agamemnon\uc1 \u8217 ?s young son, was away from Argos at the time. However, he eventually returned and, with help from his sister Electra, murdered his mother Clytaemnestra and her lover Aegisthus, as revenge for the killing of their father. The play opens a few days after that double murder.}{\fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbch \af2 \hich \af4 \loch \f4 \lang4105 \langnp4105 \langfe2052 \langfenp2052 \cf0 \par }\pard \plain \li0 \ri0 \lisb0 \lisa0 \widctlpar \aspalpha \aspnum \adjustright \lin0 \rin0 \itap0 \fs24 \rtlch \ltrch \dbc